okay today I'm going to talk to you  about drug development now in order to  get new drugs onto the market in order  to treat people who are unwell there  needs to be lots of tests that happen to  make sure that it's safe for people  taking that drug so what we're going to  look at today is we're going to look at  the different stages involved what's the  point of each of those stages and also a  little bit about blind and double-blind  trials as well so to begin with in drug  development there's kind of two  completely different sections one is  preclinical testing and one is clinical  testing so preclinical testing just  means before they're in pit before the  drug is tested with people and clinical  testing involves testing drugs on people  so the very first stage of drug  development is testing it in cells so  tissue culture in labs so what you do is  you get drugs and you put them onto  different cells so you could get some  human cells here and you see other cell  other cells damaged by the drug the  cells responding how they should do when  this drug is added so at the moment no  one has been harmed so on to the second  step if the first step they've seen  positive results in working themselves  and tissue culture then the second step  you test the drugs on animals now a lot  of people disagree with these this stage  they think it's unfair because the  animals can't give consent I can't say  it's okay but the drugs are tested on  animals again the whole point of testing  the drug on animals is to see if the  drug is toxic and to what extent is it  toxic and to get an idea of dosage so  that's preclinical testing because we  haven't tested on any people yet the  next test is to test it number three on  healthy people or healthy volunteer  even so people volunteer to have the  drug tested on them so the reason why we  test on healthy people rather than  people who are ill its first of all if  they if they were ill they might they  might be weakened and if there's any bad  side effects it might kill them or  really hurt them secondly if the  person's ill it would be difficult to  tell if the side effects are caused by  the drug or by their illness so the  point of testing and healthy volunteers  is in order to find any side effects and  also to find the optimum dosage the dose  of the level of that drug where it works  the best  now once you've tested it on healthy  volunteers our next step is then to test  it on ill volunteers so now we see if  the drug actually works how it's  supposed to so here's someone ill in bed  no thermometer right so we then tested  in ill volunteers in order to see if it  actually works  does it actually do what it's set out to  do  does it help this person who's ill with  a certain disease and the final one once  all of that stuff has gone through  remember this is clinical testing  because it's with people once this is  all gone through the final thing that  needs to happen is it needs to be  approved by a medical agency so that  just means someone reviews or rather an  agency reviews all of this information  that they've collected about the drug at  every single step and they say right is  it safe to sell to the general public  and only once it's been approved then we  can sell to the general public however  we need to know a little bit more about  this stage here about clinical trials  how do they work so clinical testing is  kind of works a bit like this so you  have a doctor so here's our doctor right  here and what they do is they have they  have two different groups  so you've got two different volunteers  because basically the idea is you want  to see if this drug works so you're  going to give the drug to one person and  the o'the said one person is going to be  given the drug the other person is in a  control group so the point of control  group is to see if there's any  difference between the drug group and  the control group to make sure the drugs  doing something however we can't just  give the drug to to the drug group and  nothing to the control group because of  this thing called the placebo effect now  what the placebo effect is this person  might just think they're getting better  because they've taken something it's  like a psychosomatic effect so what we  do is we give this person in this  control group a placebo so we give them  a pill that's just like this drug it  kind of tastes the same it's sugary the  same size everything like that but only  it doesn't have the active ingredient in  so placebo it's like a dummy drug now  what we've got to do is you've got to  make sure that that the the two people  don't know which one is in is taking the  drug and which one's taking the placebo  this is called a blind trial so the  patients don't know whether they're in  the drug group or they're in the control  group now that's kind of one way we  actually go a step further because the  doctor if they know which group is which  they might be influenced subconsciously  on their results as well so what we  actually have is something called a  double-blind trial now what this is is  neither the patient or the doctor know  which group is the drug group and which  group is the perceiver group  so just to summarize just the summarize  before the different states involved its  preclinical it's before it gets the  people they test it in a lab on cells  and tissue culture they then if it's  safe test it on animals or if it's  successful they taste it on animals to  find the dose dosage of the toxicity  then they grieve runs of clinical trials  clinical trials remember they can either  be well ideally they're a double-blind  trial where neither that the person  taking the drug no the control group  knows which ones which if it's  successful they test it on ill  volunteers to see if it works and  finally gets improved by medical agency  and we could sell it in order to try and  help people around the world thank you
 

